# Parallel worktrees and stacked PRs

One **project / issue / feature** → one **git worktree** + one **GitHub branch**.
That keeps the main checkout clean so subagents can implement unrelated work in
parallel without fighting the same index.

**Integration branch (until first release):** `initial-development`.
Create worktrees and open PRs against that branch, not `main`.

## Starting work

Before editing for a new topic:

1. Create a worktree under `.worktrees/<short-slug>/` (already gitignored) from
   latest `origin/initial-development`.
2. Create a focused branch (`feat/…`, `fix/…`, `chore/…`) in that worktree.
3. Do all implementation, commits, and verification **in that worktree**.
4. Launch parallel subagents on **different** worktrees/branches when tasks are
   independent. Do not share one dirty tree across concurrent agents.

```
# ❌ BAD — two agents editing the integration branch at once
# ✅ GOOD
git fetch origin
git worktree add .worktrees/mise-catalog -b feat/mise-catalog origin/initial-development
# agent A works there; agent B gets another worktree/branch
```

Prefer the repo’s `using-git-worktrees` skill / native worktree helpers when
available. Skip creating a second worktree if you are **already** on an
isolated worktree for that same topic.

Trivial one-line doc/typo fixes on an existing PR branch do not need a new
worktree. Anything that could collide with parallel work does.

## Pushing and stacked PRs — use `gh stack`

Install once: `gh extension install github/gh-stack` (docs: https://gh.io/stacks).

When the user asks to push / open PRs for related work:

1. Push branches with `gh stack push` (or `git push -u` then stack).
2. **Do not** manually retarget PR bases or rely on a checklist of “stacks on
   #N” alone. Use **`gh stack`** so GitHub tracks the stack and rebases upper
   PRs when a lower layer changes.
3. Trunk for stacks is **`initial-development`** until a release cut says
   otherwise (`--base initial-development`). Do not target `main` yet.
4. One PR per branch. Keep PR bodies accurate, but the stack of record is
   what `gh stack` created on GitHub—not a prose checklist.

### New stack (local tracking)

```
# bottom → top; trunk = initial-development
gh stack init --base initial-development chore/schema feat/wiring feat/ux
# …commits on each layer…
gh stack submit --auto --open
```

### Existing open PRs (no local metadata yet)

```
# bottom → top by PR number or branch name
gh stack link --base initial-development 5 3 2 6 7
```

### Keep the stack healthy

```
gh stack sync      # pull remote + rebase stack on trunk / lower layers
gh stack rebase    # rebase layers after trunk or bottom moves
gh stack view      # show local/remote stack
```

After landing or changing the bottom PR, run `gh stack sync` (or rebase) so
dependents pick up the new base—do not leave upper PRs on a stale merge-base.

### Stack order (bottom → top)

Put **infrastructure first**, **concrete product detail last**:

1. Tooling / repo layout / shared libs / CI / rules / catalog schema
2. Wiring (modules, tasks, Home Manager, generated config)
3. Call-site behavior, UX, docs that describe the finished feature

```
# ❌ BAD — feature PR first, shared catalog helper stacked on top
# ❌ BAD — only a PR-body checklist (“stacks on #5”) without gh stack
# ✅ GOOD — gh stack link/submit, base → tip
#   initial-development
#   ← chore/non-nix-catalog-schema
#     ← feat/mise-wiring
#       ← feat/llm-context-shims
```

Use `gh stack modify` / re-`link` if review reorders dependencies. Do not squash
unrelated topics into one PR to avoid stacking.
